{"id":"amorites","name":"Amorites","aliases":["Amorites"],"sources":["nave","torrey","easton"],"topics":["nave:amorites","torrey:amorites-the"],"types":[{"type":"group","from":"UBS: Names of Groups"}],"standing":"multi-index","indexes":["nave","torrey"],"thin":false,"groups":[],"definition":{"source":"easton","term":"Amorites","text":"Highlanders, or hillmen, the name given to the descendants of one of the sons of Canaan (Gen. 14:7), called Amurra or Amurri in the Assyrian and Egyptian inscriptions. On the early Babylonian monuments all Syria, including Palestine, is known as “the land of the Amorites.” The southern slopes of the mountains of Judea are called the “mount of the Amorites” (Deut. 1:7, 19, 20). They seem to have originally occupied the land stretching from the heights west of the Dead Sea (Gen. 14:7) to Hebron (13. Comp. 13:8; Deut. 3:8; 4:46-48), embracing “all Gilead and all Bashan” (Deut. 3:10), with the Jordan valley on the east of the river (4:49), the land of the “two kings of the Amorites,” Sihon and Og (Deut. 31:4; Josh. 2:10; 9:10). The five kings of the Amorites were defeated with great slaughter by Joshua (10:10). They were again defeated at the waters of Merom by Joshua, who smote them till …"},"lemmas":[{"strong":"H567","lemma":"אֱמֹרִי","xlit":"ʼĔmôrîy","gloss":"an Emorite, one of the Canaanitish tribes","kjv":"Amorite","count":57,"share":0.31},{"strong":"H3427","lemma":"יָשַׁב","xlit":"yâshab","gloss":"to sit down","kjv":"abide, continue, dwell, ease self","count":23,"share":0.13},{"strong":"H5892","lemma":"עִיר","xlit":"ʻîyr","gloss":"a city in the widest sense","kjv":"Ai, city, court, town","count":22,"share":0.12}],"verses":["Gen.10.16","Gen.14.7","Gen.14.13","Gen.15.16","Gen.15.21","Gen.48.22","Exod.3.8","Exod.3.17","Exod.33.2","Exod.34.11","Num.13.29","Num.21.13","Num.21.21","Num.21.25","Num.21.26","Num.21.29","Num.21.31","Num.21.32","Num.21.34","Num.32.33","Num.32.39","Deut.1.4","Deut.1.7","Deut.1.19","Deut.1.20","Deut.3.8","Deut.3.